WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:08.750
The concept of Covalent Bonds is what we have
studied in detail in the

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:10.080
previous video.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.640
We also concluded with a question asking what
type of

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.189
covalent bond is shared by carbon with other
atoms.

00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:22.109
Is it a single or a double or even a triple
covalent bond?

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:25.710
How does the bond
formation by carbon take place?

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:30.560
Let us answer all the queries related to bond
formation by carbon in this video.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.660
Let us begin with understanding the simple
structure of a carbon atom first.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.180
As we know, it has four valence electrons.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.640
That means sharing of all four
electrons is possible.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.410
Thus, the valency of carbon is the most important
factor

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.989
for its amazing capacity to form bonds!

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:54.010
So if we have a monovalent element like Hydrogen,
how will the bond

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.289
formation take pace?

00:00:55.289 --> 00:01:00.140
Wait a second….what do we mean by monovalent?

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.949
We
know Monos is one, and valent here refers

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:04.949
to valency!

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:07.890
That means elements
having the valency one.

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.340
It needs one electron for stability!

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.020
Coming back to our
discussion, how will the bond be formed?

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.190
This single carbon atom can share its four
valence electrons with four different

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.150
hydrogen atoms!

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.660
It makes sure that each hydrogen atom attains
stability by

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.310
having two electrons in its shell and even
carbon gets eight electrons in the

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.050
outermost shell.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.220
How?

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.520
One electron from each hydrogen atom makes
four and

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.390
there are already four valence electrons with
carbon!

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.810
These sum up to attain
the octet state.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.310
This is how a Methane molecule is formed!

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.810
A single carbon atom bonded with
four hydrogen atoms!

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:53.350
Each bond here is a single covalent bond.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.780
This is how

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.280
single covalent bond formation between carbon
and any monovalent element

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.940
takes place.

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:05.960
Now let us talk about the double and triple
covalent bonds formed by carbon.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.250
To understand this, we take a simple example
of the compound carbon dioxide

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.330
first.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.950
Can you draw the typical molecular structure
of carbon dioxide?

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:16.100
Yes!

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.610
It appears somewhat like this!

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:22.870
Two oxygen molecules bonded with one
carbon atom.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:24.281
How is this bond formed by the way?

00:02:24.281 --> 00:02:30.180
We know that the valency
of oxygen is two while that of carbon is four.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.170
That means out of the four valence electrons
two electrons will be shared with

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:39.720
one oxygen atom while the remaining two with
the second oxygen atom.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.240
But sharing means both the atoms will participate
in the bond formation right?

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.760
That is why at the same time each oxygen atom
also shares two electrons with

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.660
carbon atom.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.120
Such type of sharing enables the atoms to
attain stability.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.500
Wait a
second…did you notice that there are four

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.650
electrons or simply two pairs of
electrons shared between each oxygen and carbon

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:04.650
atom?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:05.650
Yes!

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.840
That makes it a double covalent bond right?

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:09.959
Absolutely!

00:03:09.959 --> 00:03:15.680
Now lastly, we take the example of a triple
covalent interaction of carbon.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.000
Have you ever come across the compound Hydrogen
cyanide?

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Yes!

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.569
It is an extremely poisonous liquid used for
experiments in laboratories.

00:03:24.569 --> 00:03:28.650
Do you know what its chemical composition
is?

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.030
It is HCN.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.940
So do you know what the structure looks like?

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.690
It looks like this!

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:35.800
Yes!

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:39.220
One carbon atom attached to one hydrogen atom
and one nitrogen

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:40.450
atom.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:44.550
Now if you notice, the valency of hydrogen
is one; that means one

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.450
electron of carbon is shared with this hydrogen
atom.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:52.710
Moving to the other side, the electronic configuration
of nitrogen is 2 comma

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:54.050
5.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.670
That means it needs three electrons to complete
its octet.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.670
Right?

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.860
And we
have the carbon atom waiting to share its

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.470
three electrons!

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:06.810
But we know that
sharing involves electrons from both the atoms.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:11.370
That is the reason why even
nitrogen contributes three electrons for participating

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:12.980
in the bond formation.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:18.199
Remember, a covalent bond always requires
SHARING of electrons.

00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:22.749
So that is how six electrons are shared between
these two atoms.

00:04:22.749 --> 00:04:26.409
Thus, three
pairs of electrons gives rise to a triple

00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:29.330
covalent bond between the carbon and
nitrogen atoms!

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:34.559
Now this was how carbon forms various covalent
bonds with other elements.

00:04:34.559 --> 00:04:38.710
Is this the reason why carbon forms so many
compounds?

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:42.460
Well, this is
definitely one of the major reasons why carbon

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:44.939
compounds are widely found!

00:04:44.939 --> 00:04:47.320
So what could be another reason?

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:52.389
One extremely important property of
carbon is to form bonds with ITSELF!

00:04:52.389 --> 00:04:53.389
Yes!

00:04:53.389 --> 00:04:58.029
A single carbon atom can form multiple bonds
with other carbon atoms to give

00:04:58.029 --> 00:05:00.990
rise to massive carbon chains!

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.110
This property is called Catenation.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:07.589
Let us understand the property of Catenation
in the upcoming video!

